PRIVATE REFLECTION: The question is which of the listed functions has never been an attested function of the French circumflex.

The discussion is split between F “To make a word appear more prestigious” and D “To distinguish between short and long vowel sounds”.

Most colleagues 1,2,3,5 argue F is the non-attested one. Their case:
* The circumflex is an etymological/historical phonological marker.
* Attested historical functions are:
  H – loss of a post-vocalic sibilant in spelling and speech, e.g. forêt < forest, hôte < hoste, bête < beste;
  D – original Old French vowel length marker, the compensatory lengthening that follows loss of consonant;
  A – modern open/closed contrast, ê /ɛ/ vs é /e/, â, ô etc.;
  B – homophone/homograph disambiguation, mur/mûr, du/dû, sur/sûr, ou/où, la/là;
  G and I – monophthongisation of an Old French diphthong and resolution of hiatus to a single long vowel;
  C – the inherited Latin long ō reflex written ô, i.e. Latinate [o/ɔ] words.
* No historical codification or philological description treats the accent as a stylistic/prestige ornament; any “learned look” is a by-product, not a documented orthographic function.

Minority colleagues 4,6,7 claim D is never attested, saying French never used the circumflex systematically to mark short vs long vowels and that length is only a secondary consequence. Colleague 7 also inconsistently notes prestige as a historical use while still picking D.

My current assessment: D is historically attested as the original medieval function of the circumflex to mark vowel length/compensatory lengthening. The short/long description is the classic textbook origin of the sign, even if modern French is not length-contrasting. F, by contrast, is a folk sociolinguistic perception with no attested orthographic prescription.

Remaining uncertainty: the exact phrasing of C “To indicate a vowel pronounced as [o] within words originating in Classical Latin” is a simplification of the Latin ō reflex; it overlaps with H/D and may be descriptively true for many ô cases, but is not a distinct independent function. No colleague has shown a clear counter-example that C is unattested.

Overall leaning is to F as the never-attested function, with the D-never-attested position appearing mistaken on historical grounds.

