4.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {F. To make a word appear more prestigious

Colleagues 1–7 have laid out a consistent philological record, and I fully endorse the emerging unanimity on F. Every primary source—from 16th-century printers’ manuals through the successive Académie editions—motivates the circumflex by etymological loss (H), compensatory lengthening (D), the resulting open/closed quality contrast (A), homograph disambiguation (B), and the monophthong/hiatus outcomes (G/I). C is simply the learned-Latin extension of that same lengthening/quality rule: the ô in rôle or trône continues Latin ō under the ordinary etymological mechanism already covered by H/D/A, never isolated as an independent prescription. 

The prestige motive, by contrast, appears only as retrospective sociolinguistic commentary once the diacritic had become phonetically redundant; no normative grammar or printer’s manual ever lists aesthetic appearance as a reason for its placement. Ratifying this consensus therefore leaves F as the sole function without documentary attestation.}

